WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:12.642
Hi! Sometimes washing dries in one afternoon, and sometimes it is still damp after a whole day. What affects how fast water evaporates? Let's find out!

2
00:00:12.792 --> 00:00:22.626
First, the temperature of the surroundings. The higher the temperature, the faster the evaporation. That's why clothes dry quickly on a hot, sunny day.

3
00:00:22.776 --> 00:00:30.570
Second, wind. Moving air carries the water vapour away from the wet surface, so the water evaporates faster.

4
00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:37.506
Third, the exposed surface area. Clothes spread out dry faster than clothes rolled into a ball.

5
00:00:37.656 --> 00:00:50.418
Fourth, humidity, which is how much water vapour is in the air. The drier the air, the faster the evaporation. On a rainy day the air is very humid, so clothes dry slowly.

6
00:00:50.568 --> 00:00:59.466
Let's test the effect of surface area. We pour 100 millilitres of water into a wide plate, and the same amount into a cup.

7
00:00:59.616 --> 00:01:09.522
The water in the plate has a large surface exposed to the air, so it evaporates faster. The cup has a small surface, so it evaporates more slowly.

8
00:01:09.672 --> 00:01:19.938
To keep it fair, both have the same amount of water and sit in the same place, with the same temperature, wind and humidity. We change only the surface area.

9
00:01:20.088 --> 00:01:28.530
After two days, measure how much water is left. The one with less water left evaporated faster.

10
00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:34.026
To dry clothes fastest, spread them out and hang them somewhere sunny and windy.

11
00:01:34.176 --> 00:01:41.898
A hair dryer blows warm, moving air, so hair dries quickly. It uses two factors: temperature and wind.

12
00:01:42.048 --> 00:01:48.738
On a cool, cloudy, still and humid day, puddles take a long time to dry.

13
00:01:48.888 --> 00:02:00.138
Watch out! To test the effect of wind, suppose dish A has 50 millilitres with a fan, and dish B has 100 millilitres with no fan. That's not fair!

14
00:02:00.288 --> 00:02:07.146
Both the amount of water and the wind are different. A might dry faster just because it had less water.

15
00:02:07.296 --> 00:02:14.370
In a fair test, use the same amount of water and change only one factor at a time.

16
00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:26.226
Your turn! Two equally wet towels: one is spread out and one is folded. Which dries faster? Pause and think.

17
00:02:26.376 --> 00:02:34.578
The spread-out towel dries faster, because more of its surface is exposed to the air. Did you get it right?

18
00:02:34.728 --> 00:02:44.034
Another one! Why do clothes dry so slowly on rainy days? Pause and think.

19
00:02:44.184 --> 00:02:56.586
Because the air is very humid. It already holds a lot of water vapour, so evaporation slows down. Cooler temperatures play a part too. Did you get it right?

20
00:02:56.736 --> 00:03:07.050
Last one! To test how temperature affects evaporation, what must stay the same? Pause and think.

21
00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:18.402
The amount of water, the size and shape of the container, the wind and the humidity must all stay the same. Change only the temperature. Did you get it right?

22
00:03:18.552 --> 00:03:30.354
Remember: higher temperature, more wind, a larger exposed surface and drier air all make evaporation faster. In an experiment, change only one factor at a time.

23
00:03:30.504 --> 00:03:35.466
Next time you help hang the washing, try these tips!

